{"agent":"lane-cn-submodule","data":{"by":"Qwen/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP8","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":2,"detail":"When `[submodules].verify_tree = \"refuse\"`, a worktree whose submodule state cannot be listed is returned as a plain warning string instead of an `O.refused` outcome. For example, if the bound worktree’s `.git` pointer is broken or `git submodule status` fails, `SS.detect(tree)` returns `None`; `tree_check` then returns `f\"submodule check not run in {tree}: ...\"`. The caller treats a `str` as a warning (as `claim_warnings` does) and does not undo the claim, so a claim against a worktree with uninitialized/broken submodules remains active despite the configured `refuse` mode.","digest":"a25f571d772ad910","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/api/lifecycle/claim_submodules.py:tree_check (line 34)","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"HIGH","title":"ddflow/api/lifecycle/claim_submodules.py:tree_check (line 34)"},{"chunk":2,"detail":"In `[submodules].cross_lease = \"refuse\"` mode, a detection failure is downgraded to a non-fatal `Note`. If `SS.detect(ctx.repo)` returns `None` because git cannot list submodules (e.g., corrupt git metadata, missing git binary, unreadable `.git`), `_check_submodules` returns a `Note` rather than `O.refused`. Thus a lease whose globs actually cross a submodule is allowed through with only a warning, even though the knob description says `refuse` turns the claim away.","digest":"f07293786b6977fc","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/api/lifecycle/claim_guard_submodules.py:_check_submodules (line 30)","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/api/lifecycle/claim_guard_submodules.py:_check_submodules (line 30)"},{"chunk":3,"detail":"When `SS.detect(tree)` returns `None` (git cannot list submodules), the function calls `O.nothing(\"submodules\", f\"git could not list...\", text=\"\", submodules=[])`. In this module the other outcome constructors use `text` for the human-readable outcome text (`O.ok(..., text=...)` and `O.failed(..., text)`), so the second positional argument to `O.nothing` binds the same `text` parameter that is then supplied again as the keyword `text=\"\"`. Given a repository where `git submodule status` fails (e.g. corrupt gitdir), the intended exit-2 “could not tell” path raises `TypeError` instead of returning that outcome.","digest":"69c24ad21f271aed","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/api/submodules.py:28","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/api/submodules.py:28"},{"chunk":4,"detail":"The submodule path is treated as a literal prefix in `_is_inside` but is passed unescaped as a glob pattern to `GL.overlap` in `_spans`. Given a submodule whose path literally contains a glob metacharacter, e.g. `vendor/?`, the lease glob `vendor/?/**` is classified as inside because `_is_inside` sees the literal prefix `vendor/?/` and returns no crossing. But as a glob, `?` matches any single character, so the same glob also matches paths outside the submodule such as `vendor/x/file`; that is a crossing and should be refused. The same lack of escaping can also produce false crossings: for submodule `vendor/?`, `GL.overlap(\"vendor/x/file\", \"vendor/?/**\")` treats the literal `?` in the submodule path as a wildcard and reports a crossing for a glob that is outside the submodule. This can silently mis-scope a lease across a submodule boundary.","digest":"14245c161b31bf98","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/core/submodules.py:_is_inside/_spans","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/core/submodules.py:_is_inside/_spans"},{"chunk":5,"detail":"`cross_refusal` assumes there is only one crossed submodule. It sorts the crossed submodules and uses only `subs[0]` in the suggested fix: `ddflow update {item}-inner --globs {first}/**` and `ddflow update {item}-outer --globs <the rest>`. If a claim crosses two or more submodules, e.g. item globs `**` with submodules `a` and `b`, the generated commands still leave `b` in the outer item’s lease, so the outer lease would still cross a submodule boundary. That contradicts the docstring’s promise that the words name “the calls that get past it”.","digest":"d86e25137c55defc","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/services/submodule_text.py:42","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/submodule_text.py:42"},{"chunk":5,"detail":"The shell commands generated by `cross_refusal` interpolate `item` and `first` without quoting or escaping. Git submodule paths may contain spaces. For a submodule path `my sub`, the refusal tells the user to run `ddflow update item-inner --globs my sub/**`, which a shell parses as two globs, `my` and `sub/**`, not the intended single glob. The suggested next call is therefore wrong for valid submodule paths containing spaces.","digest":"bf691ea4669f1b2d","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/services/submodule_text.py:42","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"LOW","title":"ddflow/services/submodule_text.py:42"},{"chunk":6,"detail":"`fix` builds shell command strings by interpolating `tree` and `self.path` directly, but the docstring calls the result “the exact next call.” Given a worktree at `/tmp/my repo` and an uninitialized submodule `sub`, the uninitialised branch returns `git -C /tmp/my repo submodule update --init --recursive`; run in a shell, `git -C` sees `/tmp/my` as the worktree and `repo` as another argument, so the command fails. The pointer branch has the same problem for `rm {Path(tree) / self.path / '.git'}`, and unquoted paths can also execute shell metacharacters if a caller treats the returned string as a command.","digest":"f601a1aceb4a56af","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/services/submodules.py:Problem.fix","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/submodules.py:Problem.fix"},{"chunk":8,"detail":"The tree-check description contradicts the knob description. Line 34 says a failing tree check causes the claim to be undone, but line 37 says `verify_tree=warn` claims and only prints a warning. Concrete input: operator sets `ddflow config submodules.verify_tree warn --local`, then claims an item whose submodule is not initialised. The document simultaneously specifies that the claim is undone and that the claim proceeds with a warning, leaving the intended behavior ambiguous.","digest":"9add1272ba60812d","family":"alibaba","location":"docs/guide/submodules.md:34","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"docs/guide/submodules.md:34"},{"chunk":10,"detail":"The test treats a lease whose only glob is `[\"vendor/lib\"]` as “wholly inside” the submodule and asserts `CS.crossings([\"vendor/lib\"], [SUB]) == []`. But `vendor/lib` is the submodule path itself, which in Git is a parent-repository gitlink entry, not a file or directory wholly inside the submodule. A task/claim scoped to `vendor/lib` can therefore pass the cross-boundary guard and then stage/commit a submodule-pointer change in the parent repo without a paired submodule item, which is exactly the cross-boundary case the change says must be refused. The expectation should be a crossing/boundary refusal for the exact submodule path, not an empty crossing list.","digest":"1dbe663bb15a33d1","family":"alibaba","location":"tests/test_submodule_detect.py:34","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"tests/test_submodule_detect.py:34"}],"chunks_total":10,"coverage":"10/10 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":45893,"diff_sha":"43f24694c0d0fd7f25503035c8f7d9287753654e2fafec1e25bf0a9da247c478","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked working-tree changes in $HOME/src/ddflow/.ddflow/worktrees/CN-submodule-detect","diff_stat":{"deletions":0,"files":0,"insertions":0,"untracked":0},"elapsed_s":732.5,"family":"alibaba","findings":9,"max_chunk_chars":5000,"m